 \appendix
+\section{The evidence process}
+\label{app:eprocess}
+
+Fix agent $i$ and theory $m$.
+At a visited pair $(C,a)$ write $q=\marg{i}m(C)(a)$ for $m$'s prediction; adequacy at $(C,a)$ is $\TV(p_i^{*}(\cdot\mid C,a),q)\le\eta$, equivalently $\lvert p_i^{*}(D)-q(D)\rvert\le\eta$ for every $D\subseteq X_i$.
+For each such $D$, each sign, and each betting fraction $\lambda\in[0,\lambda_{\max}]$, the process that multiplies by $1+\lambda\bigl(\mathbf 1\{x\in D\}-(q(D)+\eta)\bigr)$ at each visit to $(C,a)$, and by $1$ otherwise, is nonnegative and, under adequacy, a supermartingale: its conditional expectation multiplies by $1+\lambda\bigl(p_i^{*}(D)-q(D)-\eta\bigr)\le1$.
+Let $M_i^{m,(C,a)}$ average these betting processes over $D$, sign, and a prior on $\lambda$---a \emph{mixture} e-value, so no bet need be fixed in advance---and set
+\[
+E_i^{m}=\sum_{(C,a)}v_{(C,a)}\,M_i^{m,(C,a)},
+\qquad
+\textstyle\sum_{(C,a)}v_{(C,a)}=1,\ v_{(C,a)}>0.
+\]
+As a convex combination of nonnegative supermartingales, $E_i^{m}$ is a nonnegative supermartingale under the null that $m$ is adequate at every pair, with $E_i^{m}=1$ before play; Ville's inequality then gives $\Pr(\sup_t E_i^{m}\ge e^{b_i})\le e^{-b_i}$, the false-alarm bound of \cref{def:test}.
+If instead $m$ is inadequate at a recurrent pair $j^{*}=(C,a)$---some $D$ has $p_i^{*}(D)>q(D)+\eta$---the corresponding betting process has strictly positive expected log-growth, so
+\[
+E_i^{m}\ \ge\ v_{j^{*}}\,M_i^{m,j^{*}}\ \longrightarrow\ \infty\quad\text{a.s.},
+\]
+at a rate equal to that pair's optimal betting exponent (a Chernoff information divergence); this is the dominating-component property used in \cref{lem:test-consistency,prop:capacity}.
+In the exact deterministic case $\eta=0$ with degenerate predictions, the $D=\{x\}$ bet with $\lambda\to\lambda_{\max}$ reduces to the likelihood ratio, and $E_i^{m}$ becomes $+\infty$ at the first consequence $m$ deems impossible.
+
